1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,180
Put living cells under a microscope,

2
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:06,888
and a camera sees almost nothing: they're nearly transparent.

3
00:00:06,958 --> 00:00:09,085
Yet the light that went through them changed a lot.

4
00:00:09,125 --> 00:00:12,787
Not in brightness, but in phase: the cells delayed it.

5
00:00:12,857 --> 00:00:15,800
So how do you photograph something a camera can't see?

6
00:00:15,870 --> 00:00:17,014
That's the phase problem.

7
00:00:17,054 --> 00:00:20,573
Its solutions are holography and phase retrieval.

8
00:00:21,393 --> 00:00:25,625
Remember: at each point, light is an arrow, a complex number.

9
00:00:25,665 --> 00:00:28,778
Its length is the amplitude, its angle the phase.

10
00:00:28,848 --> 00:00:31,921
A cell slows light down slightly. Where it's thicker,

11
00:00:31,961 --> 00:00:35,391
the light falls further behind, so its arrow turns further.

12
00:00:35,461 --> 00:00:38,661
But a camera can't record arrows. It records brightness,

13
00:00:38,701 --> 00:00:41,832
the squared length |U|², and throws every angle away.

14
00:00:41,902 --> 00:00:44,427
Why not measure the angle? Every arrow spins:

15
00:00:44,467 --> 00:00:47,956
green light oscillates 600 trillion times a second,

16
00:00:47,996 --> 00:00:50,427
far too fast for any detector.

17
00:00:50,497 --> 00:00:54,167
So for a transparent object, all the information sits in the phase,

18
00:00:54,207 --> 00:00:56,598
the very part the camera loses.

19
00:00:58,218 --> 00:01:03,066
Dennis Gabor's idea, in 1948: add a known reference wave, R.

20
00:01:03,106 --> 00:01:07,417
It spins in step with U, so the angle between them holds still.

21
00:01:07,487 --> 00:01:10,027
The camera sees the length of the sum, U + R.

22
00:01:10,067 --> 00:01:13,652
Change U's phase, and that length grows and shrinks.

23
00:01:13,722 --> 00:01:15,975
The phase is now written in brightness.

24
00:01:16,845 --> 00:01:21,020
Expanding the square gives four terms. The two cross terms,

25
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:24,744
UR* and U*R, contain U itself, phase and all.

26
00:01:24,814 --> 00:01:27,817
With the reference straight on, as in Gabor's setup,

27
00:01:27,857 --> 00:01:30,200
all four terms pile up in one place,

28
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:32,754
and the twin, U*, spoils the picture.

29
00:01:32,824 --> 00:01:36,404
In 1962, Leith and Upatnieks tilted the reference.

30
00:01:36,444 --> 00:01:40,196
Now the camera records fine stripes, called fringes.

31
00:01:40,266 --> 00:01:44,633
A tilt is a shift in spatial frequency. So in the spectrum,

32
00:01:44,673 --> 00:01:48,009
UR* slides to one side, and its twin to the other.

33
00:01:48,829 --> 00:01:51,564
Here's a computed off-axis hologram of our cells.

34
00:01:51,604 --> 00:01:55,338
Take its Fourier transform, and three islands appear.

35
00:01:55,408 --> 00:01:57,552
The middle island holds |U|² and |R|².

36
00:01:57,592 --> 00:02:02,585
On either side sits one cross term: UR*, and its twin.

37
00:02:02,655 --> 00:02:05,923
They mustn't overlap. The middle one is twice as wide,

38
00:02:05,963 --> 00:02:08,113
so the tilt's frequency must be at least

39
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:10,192
three times the object's bandwidth.

40
00:02:10,262 --> 00:02:14,188
Now crop one island, move it to the centre, and transform back.

41
00:02:14,858 --> 00:02:16,561
Out comes the full complex field:

42
00:02:16,601 --> 00:02:20,052
brightness shows amplitude, and colour shows phase.

43
00:02:20,122 --> 00:02:22,567
We recorded this hologram out of focus.

44
00:02:22,607 --> 00:02:24,655
But with the whole field in hand,

45
00:02:24,695 --> 00:02:28,802
the angular spectrum method from episode one can propagate it anywhere.

46
00:02:28,872 --> 00:02:31,692
Sweep the distance, and the cells snap into focus,

47
00:02:31,732 --> 00:02:34,111
long after the picture was taken.

48
00:02:35,731 --> 00:02:37,618
What if there's no reference, only brightness?

49
00:02:37,658 --> 00:02:40,282
Then you lean on what you already know.

50
00:02:40,352 --> 00:02:43,051
Here, light passes through a window of known shape,

51
00:02:43,091 --> 00:02:44,953
and the sample is transparent.

52
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:48,236
So in this plane, every arrow's length is known.

53
00:02:48,306 --> 00:02:51,910
A lens shows the far field, and a camera there gives its lengths too.

54
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:54,991
Lengths in both planes, angles in neither.

55
00:02:55,061 --> 00:02:57,730
The Gerchberg–Saxton algorithm starts from random

56
00:02:57,770 --> 00:03:01,725
angles and goes back and forth with Fourier transforms.

57
00:03:01,795 --> 00:03:05,698
In each plane, it keeps the angles, but swaps in the lengths it knows.

58
00:03:05,768 --> 00:03:07,990
Each swap puts the guess on one constraint.

59
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:12,886
Bouncing between them, it homes in on a field that satisfies both.

60
00:03:12,956 --> 00:03:16,409
Watch the error fall, as the cells emerge from the noise.

61
00:03:17,279 --> 00:03:22,346
The far field alone has blind spots: a shifted copy, a flipped twin,

62
00:03:22,386 --> 00:03:25,585
or a constant phase gives exactly the same brightness.

63
00:03:25,655 --> 00:03:28,135
Our lopsided window rules out the first two.

64
00:03:28,175 --> 00:03:30,146
More diverse data helps too:

65
00:03:30,186 --> 00:03:34,607
several distances, or the overlapping scans of ptychography.

66
00:03:35,427 --> 00:03:37,666
Now run it backwards. Instead of measuring

67
00:03:37,706 --> 00:03:40,470
a far field, ask for one: say, a λ.

68
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:42,189
The mask can only change phase,

69
00:03:42,229 --> 00:03:45,584
like a liquid-crystal screen or an etched glass plate.

70
00:03:45,624 --> 00:03:48,968
Same loop, with the target in place of the measurement.

71
00:03:49,038 --> 00:03:53,251
Watch the mask's phase scramble, and the far field snap into the target.

72
00:03:53,921 --> 00:03:57,166
The grain is speckle. The target fixes only brightness,

73
00:03:57,206 --> 00:03:59,787
so the far-field phase comes out random,

74
00:03:59,827 --> 00:04:03,802
and nearby points interfere with each other, as in episode four.

75
00:04:03,872 --> 00:04:06,735
Modern designs often use gradient descent instead,

76
00:04:06,775 --> 00:04:09,060
which we'll meet in episode ten.

77
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:12,961
On a computer, each idea is just a few lines.

78
00:04:13,031 --> 00:04:16,394
Holography: FFT the hologram, crop the island,

79
00:04:16,434 --> 00:04:20,944
inverse FFT, then refocus with the angular spectrum method.

80
00:04:21,014 --> 00:04:25,530
Retrieval and design: a loop of FFTs that swaps in what you know.

81
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,768
Two pitfalls. Every fringe needs at least two pixels,

82
00:04:28,808 --> 00:04:32,979
or the island runs off the edge of the spectrum and wraps around.

83
00:04:33,049 --> 00:04:37,417
So off-axis holography spends part of the camera's pixels on fringes.

84
00:04:37,487 --> 00:04:38,984
And Gerchberg–Saxton can stall.

85
00:04:39,024 --> 00:04:43,155
Restart it from new random angles, or switch to gradients.

86
00:04:43,975 --> 00:04:47,374
Cameras lose phase. Interference writes it into fringes,

87
00:04:47,414 --> 00:04:51,119
and constraints let a computer recover it, or design it.

88
00:04:51,189 --> 00:04:53,502
Where does it break? Holography needs steady,

89
00:04:53,542 --> 00:04:56,958
coherent light, and retrieval needs diverse data.

90
00:04:57,028 --> 00:04:58,992
We just designed a phase mask on a computer.

91
00:04:59,032 --> 00:05:02,536
How do you build one, and what does "thin" really mean?

